'''Montjueu''' or '''Montjuich''' ({{Langx|ca|Montjuïc|4=Jewish Mountain}}) is a [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of the [[Hesperia|Hesperian community]] within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. It was initially settled by {{W|Sephardic Jews}}, [[Mozbayana|Mozbayáns]] and [[W:Arab-Berber|Bayani-Imfezzans]] exiled from their homes around the Mesogean basin. The legacy of Montjueu has re-emerged in the 21st century out of a desire for preservation of its culture, the [[W:Judaeo-Spanish|Judaeo-Sartan]], [[W:Judeo-Volisanian|Judeo-Volisanian]] and [[W:Judeo-Provençal|Judeo-Beron]] languages (collectively called ''Ladino''), and their intimate historical ties to the [[Mesogean World|Mesogean world]].

'''Montjueisme''' ({{lang-bl|montjueuismo}}) is a primarily [[Regionalism in Balisca|regionalist]] political movement active in the Montjueu region and surrounding areas, most of what is today considered the [[Judeo-Baliscan cultural zone]]. It seeks to highlight the influences and contributions of [[W:Judeo-Catalan|Judeo-Volisanian]], [[Judaeo-Sartan]], and [[Judeo-Beron]] settlers to the <nowiki>[[Baliscan Confederation]]</nowiki> and its society since the 16th century, as well as advocate for the preservation and recognition of the region's distinctive cultural heritage within the confederal framework. Historically speaking, Jews played a significant role in the development of early Baliscan identity, and contemporary movements seek to promote the rediscovery of Judeo-Baliscan heritage, traditions, culture, and language.

